#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546085f7-b447-560f-8e03-5f9f83d6f2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282406} {:answer/id #uuid "74f33be1-f6b9-527c-9c36-b38596ce0f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282688} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6ac22c-45ee-5e02-aa23-fa2d93a62a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282915} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8a9385-1ddd-50d1-9fa6-1227b2a8f619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282916} {:answer/id #uuid "91107c67-4b51-563a-9b9f-7ec21682f202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d6ac22c-45ee-5e02-aa23-fa2d93a62a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282915} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0740db-ef2c-573e-a13f-6da541dd04e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f247252-c196-59cb-b56b-5c53685b26d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-170668" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x={t}^{2},$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot 2t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97cfef0b-b2e9-52d1-81b0-399192df2eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383609} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd4c4de-e1e5-5ff6-83aa-88f43410589b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383610} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d046f0-8479-5ddd-8e29-9cf04c57ccd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383611} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf412ac-5ff2-5717-972e-75f25275ddc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383612} {:answer/id #uuid "9e75a459-147f-5daa-8fd7-55841122ff55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bd4c4de-e1e5-5ff6-83aa-88f43410589b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383610} :answer-field/id #uuid "614acb07-1733-5745-addd-2f741b4bd7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5274ee67-b6e6-57da-a682-d2ddd4bf1256" :question/math-academy-id "q-170669" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={e}^{2y-x},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{2y-x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{e}^{2y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125ca586-3337-5120-876d-92e40e11dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437260} {:answer/id #uuid "ba33cf18-d667-5a40-9d16-6cf17e9bc306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437263} {:answer/id #uuid "dacbed10-7956-5d58-b766-2a05b8deb073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437266} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc660c-f7e4-5ae5-93dd-d34894e5734f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437267} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3c8e0-fd65-53d1-b833-3d4e2653e49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3c8e0-fd65-53d1-b833-3d4e2653e49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437269} :answer-field/id #uuid "f729b4c9-529c-5fb2-a0e6-3a129de28dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab9cce6f-4b79-504d-bebd-6e1b2e2dc75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-170671" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8172957c-8ded-58d3-9a23-800391f6abc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383616} {:answer/id #uuid "65fc28e8-01f1-5779-81a2-95006d854621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383617} {:answer/id #uuid "78f76e21-a29b-5ac4-bef0-5e96cc36b301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{s}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383618} {:answer/id #uuid "1650585c-4f5b-5c09-98dc-1b8f36155d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}+2+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383619} {:answer/id #uuid "9d243851-27bb-5ff4-9d66-8de5b61c5110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{s}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78f76e21-a29b-5ac4-bef0-5e96cc36b301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{s}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383618} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31244f4-c1a2-50a4-adcb-bc5510fae670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04777d40-e9e1-5da3-94ee-34ffd4874f77" :question/math-academy-id "q-170674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y,$ where $x={e}^{s}+t,$ $y=s+1$ and $z=1-t-s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-1)\\cdot {e}^{s}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-{e}^{s}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e42d90a-97cf-5414-b80d-82a4862b83de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{cos}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385365} {:answer/id #uuid "016496f9-039d-53a6-96db-cf3960e6a193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385366} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6f4593-e298-5f84-b3e8-cd2d86629bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4t{sin}^{2}⁡s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385367} {:answer/id #uuid "b99ddb71-4211-5aa0-a805-5281aabf9bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385368} {:answer/id #uuid "a78b674e-3f6a-5922-89c6-75ee6f89da98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-4{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016496f9-039d-53a6-96db-cf3960e6a193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385366} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5d0091-d440-5a1e-b574-7e1631c61ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66bc23d2-d72e-5919-9b3d-d85b675dd732" :question/math-academy-id "q-37201" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2},$ where $x=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡s) \\\\  & =2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡s \\\\  & =2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17573a89-2ada-5cfd-8592-1b89b18d61f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420990} {:answer/id #uuid "3856cad9-d2c1-55a4-adf6-784ae35a6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420991} {:answer/id #uuid "5c54b913-d15a-53bc-b2c6-f71477530f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420992} {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} {:answer/id #uuid "d87585d5-b5cd-5fbc-b58c-a3d2c45f4e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f118ee24-10a3-5bda-8238-4be0365cace0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f22c7a35-af74-59c8-b71b-cae8347ea97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37206" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+{z}^{2},$ where $x={s}^{2}+{t}^{2},$ $y=\\frac{s}{t}$ and $z=s{t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+{c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}s{t}^{4},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{s}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{s}{t})\\cdot 2s+({s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2})\\cdot {t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{t}+\\frac{{s}^{2}}{t}+t+st+st+2s{t}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+t+2st+2s{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=1+2+2=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "894ca471-2dd4-5d40-b36a-8da27b160d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385372} {:answer/id #uuid "a0652b0f-d1a7-5fb6-a24a-41d904eaf606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385373} {:answer/id #uuid "290a2a7e-ad92-516f-ae90-67ba3132c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385374} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad83d4e-e307-5e90-9e4f-cc60c72f71d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385375} {:answer/id #uuid "10e3eea0-e64b-5459-8cc2-03bcb5ecde36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad83d4e-e307-5e90-9e4f-cc60c72f71d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385375} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ca2715-a0aa-5460-b75b-4a47465638ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b9407c6-12a8-5179-a6fb-edc61bbc79bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37208" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}z,$ where $x=3t,$ $y=t-s$ and $z=2st.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{c}_{1}s{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{s}^{2}t+{c}_{3}{s}^{3},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(t-s)(2st)=4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =(t-s)^{2}={t}^{2}-2st+{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\cdot 3+(4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t)\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}-2st+{s}^{2})\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t+2s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+6s{t}^{2}-8{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=6,$ ${c}_{2}=-8$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=6+(-8)+2=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7d16f8-21b4-57d7-b847-6cbf8aec3e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3t+2{t}^{4}){e}^{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478816} {:answer/id #uuid "a796782b-7f7c-5c6c-8be9-0d882da5296e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t+3{t}^{4}){e}^{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478834} {:answer/id #uuid "cae71365-386c-58a4-9a23-5f12d23921f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478884} {:answer/id #uuid "e885c723-0ce8-5f1e-ad99-55ec7b846c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+3{t}^{3}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478920} {:answer/id #uuid "fba666e9-d566-570e-bbc3-a41fcf8b4194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3t+2{t}^{3}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a796782b-7f7c-5c6c-8be9-0d882da5296e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t+3{t}^{4}){e}^{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478834} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b1652d-93ab-5313-9296-f0aa0f63c08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "284d40a1-d125-53c0-b06b-70dfdac633d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94587" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x{e}^{xy},x={t}^{2},$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{{t}^{2}\\cdot t}+{t}^{2}\\cdot t{e}^{{t}^{2}\\cdot t}={e}^{{t}^{3}}+{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{3}}=(1+{t}^{3}){e}^{{t}^{3}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =({t}^{2})^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}\\cdot t}={t}^{4}{e}^{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(1+{t}^{3}){e}^{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 2t+{t}^{4}{e}^{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(2t+2{t}^{4}){e}^{{t}^{3}}+{t}^{4}{e}^{{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =(2t+3{t}^{4}){e}^{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00add8e7-6980-56f0-9a2a-bb552536c12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357760} {:answer/id #uuid "286d61f8-a3ff-5fbf-b774-2c2cb79b67bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358115} {:answer/id #uuid "6d859cb3-66c2-535d-8f19-f1b8830867ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358708} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe84aab-93e0-5d36-b349-3a91b328cae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359385} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a2ec28-c873-5c71-8848-26eba34233c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe84aab-93e0-5d36-b349-3a91b328cae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359385} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aac33cc-3f0e-5b51-8653-40c739dd5910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ec1810-6288-5fe9-a1ed-f6d4d3a585e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94588" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a08b852d-dd88-5d77-8ddb-331167273436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378033} {:answer/id #uuid "f90bb410-5dcb-5f1b-9fb7-2aab135e836b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378034} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd0123d-db34-56ee-8e11-8923b0a7b1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378035} {:answer/id #uuid "fe429b03-fa1d-52c0-9976-c7f30c69dfc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378036} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc9cd2-e31f-514b-9f87-beb31da0389e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a08b852d-dd88-5d77-8ddb-331167273436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378033} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e3b0fb6-3358-5fd4-9918-187307cf0084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b576dec-df7c-5124-81b5-502e3d89f6ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-94589" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2xy-3{z}^{2},$ where $x=st,$ $y=s+3t,$ and $z=2s-t.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=2({s}^{2}t+{s}^{2}+{c}_{1}st+{c}_{2}s+{c}_{3}t),\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-6z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)+2(s+3t)=2st+2s+6t \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2st \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-6(2s-t)=6t-12s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(2st+2s+6t)\\cdot s+(2st)\\cdot 3+(6t-12s)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{2}+6st+6st-6t+12s \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{2}+12st+12s-6t \\\\  & =2({s}^{2}t+{s}^{2}+6st+6s-3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}=6,{c}_{3}=-3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=6+6-3=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5011f4d0-0c40-5764-9ee2-dff49de39782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437261} {:answer/id #uuid "639601fb-cc44-52a3-bcb1-718c744fd6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437262} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb75712-99aa-5d77-a9d1-0d236df66a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437264} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} {:answer/id #uuid "f132c264-24c8-5558-941c-11fddb4def73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c152aa95-4d94-5d9c-b5bb-30ac91e12518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00d74228-1684-517b-8b3d-a3ad6a19bdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010a1725-78d9-5ef6-976d-66b572c1b87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ts({t}^{2}-3ts+2{s}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385826} {:answer/id #uuid "3a12b2ee-d263-5ffd-96bf-c7a89479c594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{t}^{2}(s-2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385827} {:answer/id #uuid "cd01d3dd-a324-5561-83a5-466a5fba3616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({s}^{2}+s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385828} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3eb310-caaa-5a1e-9eea-e12deb4203bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ts(1+ts)(1-st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385829} {:answer/id #uuid "39c971fc-9574-59c2-93dc-706cf5f69912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{s}^{2}(2s-3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c971fc-9574-59c2-93dc-706cf5f69912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{s}^{2}(2s-3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830} :answer-field/id #uuid "722f5fc1-8625-5de3-b976-ddcdf5183ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cddd99f6-007e-52c0-bac7-f496a002a8cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94748" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2},$ where $x=ts$ and $y=s-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(ts)(s-t)=2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(ts)^{2}={t}^{2}{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2})(s)+({t}^{2}{s}^{2})(-1) \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-2{s}^{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-3{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =t{s}^{2}(2s-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c7ed61-be76-5c28-943e-73d839de469b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378040} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c7131-1236-565f-820c-71d0c9d6461f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378041} {:answer/id #uuid "81e6772a-0701-5fba-976e-107b1b9bdbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({e}^{2s}-{e}^{-2s})}{{e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378042} {:answer/id #uuid "96f1087d-82ca-53f2-b58c-87296e971578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s}}{2({e}^{2s}-{e}^{-2s})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378043} {:answer/id #uuid "99ca07b6-ae62-53fa-8034-91167ff2ab00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6c7ed61-be76-5c28-943e-73d839de469b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378040} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12f54b9-7bd3-5a99-bf1e-a42a31564b96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b3dcc80-0abe-5373-bdc1-9e25975b2bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94751" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $x=t{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{-s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2(t{e}^{s})}{(t{e}^{s})^{2}+(t{e}^{-s})^{2}}=\\frac{2{e}^{s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2(t{e}^{-s})}{(t{e}^{s})^{2}+(t{e}^{-s})^{2}}=\\frac{2{e}^{-s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{-s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}\\cdot {e}^{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}\\cdot {e}^{-s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d654e2f-f3fc-5258-a49f-2d34c0fe2e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478630} {:answer/id #uuid "43c4c48d-421f-5939-a191-17a25a99baca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478652} {:answer/id #uuid "629974f2-9b87-5ba0-9a3b-689b12569330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478701} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c2f672-c569-5344-b958-fc7c3946d8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b45a28-1354-55e5-892b-95b377da47a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d654e2f-f3fc-5258-a49f-2d34c0fe2e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478630} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e05c6e0-2566-5a04-96b0-62a33e1b84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d50078aa-bdfd-5339-beac-094a7cdda30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94752" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ where $x=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =({e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+({e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fc4780-527f-5686-a54d-abb89b220c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357832} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b12e0c-b95b-5473-906d-837cd6ca25f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t+4s-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359338} {:answer/id #uuid "d526a50b-7e3f-5fc3-bb9e-6e2833f01728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t-s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359615} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa5887a-199b-5b40-8eff-86703cb3bfe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359841} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfb289f-dfba-5fc0-a653-13f7660f68c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t-s-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08fc4780-527f-5686-a54d-abb89b220c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357832} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4f8771-2072-5512-a9d1-948309d3d440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4381d9a9-c913-5a57-ad60-d264f0f8dbf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94811" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=3x-2y+z,$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}-{t}^{2}$ and $z=s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2t)+(1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181e483e-0db4-5bd1-964b-afa6fc2ecf27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357963} {:answer/id #uuid "719dcb03-d975-5054-b48a-74dd57cccf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358745} {:answer/id #uuid "76fada7e-bbdf-508e-9127-190df8ac22e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358776} {:answer/id #uuid "81dec9c1-74ca-5c5c-9366-b27ffa80a962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358886} {:answer/id #uuid "c17c2336-706c-5cd5-8b88-2ef24b3288c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "719dcb03-d975-5054-b48a-74dd57cccf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358745} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee046ec2-08f8-5c58-bfb3-059a2ba841f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bb11d2f-2294-5c9e-b733-36535d04471b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95079" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x=4t$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdc706a-1707-5c09-affb-248453b8b3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+{s}^{2}}{1+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357857} {:answer/id #uuid "154db3be-7d69-5ebc-979b-f406345e0c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s(1-s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357939} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a21b-0849-5652-a19d-cd8b9c45a0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}(2+s)}{(1+s)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358001} {:answer/id #uuid "7cec9aba-8f27-55a0-917c-38e83d5f0aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358838} {:answer/id #uuid "e28edfdb-62cd-569f-b9f0-d31cb58213f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e28edfdb-62cd-569f-b9f0-d31cb58213f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359756} :answer-field/id #uuid "083d442a-f627-5528-9841-283282543ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a57f510-aa58-5248-9a92-0fa5039eed64" :question/math-academy-id "q-95085" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+y},$ $x={s}^{2}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{1+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{(1+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{1+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+s}\\cdot 2s-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{1+s}-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s(1+s)-{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13df3da6-fa03-593a-b697-020ee592a4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357924} {:answer/id #uuid "205aa05e-a7f6-5abf-9311-2b838c211938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358036} {:answer/id #uuid "682e2303-0eea-58c6-9296-93c2403f441a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358663} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e70a37-4f39-508f-a9c2-c0d746a9d541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359443} {:answer/id #uuid "fa934368-527c-591b-bae3-d56a4c98c73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(1-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682e2303-0eea-58c6-9296-93c2403f441a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358663} :answer-field/id #uuid "97ecada2-93d5-5ee3-a2ef-d0b7948dd606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25aad332-4985-5005-bb5a-87ea911c6d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-95117" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d5ecadd-1d99-51f3-b3ee-0d0a52a7e504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478669} {:answer/id #uuid "7eececa3-aed5-56bc-8acd-83a7d9f52ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+2{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478748} {:answer/id #uuid "c04864f4-8e0e-5d9d-8763-0a9d20cfc1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478870} {:answer/id #uuid "c22286df-0bd2-569b-80cb-dd78460f6e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478871} {:answer/id #uuid "fd87021b-423b-576b-b08a-47b84980a27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd87021b-423b-576b-b08a-47b84980a27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478956} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1b30ab-e87a-541b-926a-088f06d1391d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "889d0bfa-8294-56c7-a3ce-0b0d572aff69" :question/math-academy-id "q-95120" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={e}^{2x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e4af3f-61bc-5126-83bf-edde59b61b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478611} {:answer/id #uuid "3240fcea-f2a6-504a-8d73-e1a71cf1a1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478635} {:answer/id #uuid "7dae59e3-fbbc-543f-9d25-16ccca4d11a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478747} {:answer/id #uuid "acf73fac-21b4-5b22-a6ba-f45bb952aa13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478842} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e3aeda-9359-5ed2-b5a4-5bdf7646a57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e4af3f-61bc-5126-83bf-edde59b61b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478611} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f7da20-bbae-51ed-a0e8-82abd9017a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "192e8101-5b84-55a9-943c-4057db856b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95123" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy-4}$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy-4}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy-4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x\\cdot {x}^{2}-4}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{x\\cdot {x}^{2}-4}}=\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}+\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
